-- title:
--   Maximal order at a division place is the nrd valuation ring
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ and let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of the rational quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ which is a maximal order in the following sense: $\Lambda$ contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, its $\mathbb{Q}$-span is all of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ and it is finitely generated as a $\mathbb{Z}$-module, and every submodule with these four properties that contains $\Lambda$ is equal to $\Lambda$. Let $v$ be a height-one prime of the ring of integers of $\mathbb{Q}$, and assume that every nonzero element of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ is a unit, i.e. this algebra is a division ring; write $p$ for the prime number attached to $v$ by `Rat.HeightOneSpectrum.primesEquiv`. The assertion is that there exists a ring isomorphism $\Theta$ from $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ onto $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}_p,a,b]$ (the coefficients being the images of $a$ and $b$ in $\mathbb{Q}_p$) such that, first, $\Theta(1\otimes c)$ is the scalar multiple of $1$ by the image of $c$ under the canonical isomorphism $\mathbb{Q}_v\cong\mathbb{Q}_p$, for every $c\in\mathbb{Q}_v$, and second, the additive subgroup [`Submodule.localBox`](def/Submodule_LocalBox.html#L40) of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ generated by the elements $z\otimes c$ with $z\in\Lambda$ and $c$ in the valuation ring of $\mathbb{Q}_v$ has, as a set, exactly the elements $x$ with $\lVert\operatorname{nrd}(\Theta x)\rVert\le 1$, where $\operatorname{nrd}(y)=y_{\mathrm{re}}^2-a\,y_{i}^2-b\,y_{j}^2+ab\,y_{k}^2$.
--
--   This is the local description of a maximal order at a finite place where the quaternion algebra is a division algebra: after identifying the completion with $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}_p,a,b]$, the local completion of the order is the unique maximal order, namely the valuation ring cut out by $\lVert\operatorname{nrd}\rVert\le 1$. It is used to produce order coordinates in the Čerednik–Drinfeld setting and to derive the statements that an element of a maximal order whose reduced norm has $p$-adic valuation at least $2$ is a natural-number multiple of an element of the order.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_QuaternionAlgebra_IsMaximalOrder_exists_ringEquiv_coe_localBox_eq_setOf_norm_nrd_le_one_of_forall_isUnit.lean

